{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Northern Pacific's descent off the Palouse into Lewiston -- the line that brought Lewiston, Idaho its first railroad. The Spokane and Palouse Railway, an NP predecessor incorporated in 1886, pushed south from the NP main line at Marshall (near Spokane) through Pullman and Moscow, reaching Genesee, Idaho in 1888 and building a branch on to Juliaetta in 1891. The last stretch, Juliaetta down the Potlatch and Clearwater rivers into Lewiston, was held up for years by right-of-way questions across the Nez Perce Reservation; the NP finally completed it and the first train whistled into Lewiston on September 15 1898. The S&P itself was sold to the Northern Pacific on February 21 1899. The route is deliberately circuitous -- it runs east from Moscow to Troy, drops southeast to Kendrick, then doubles back west down the Potlatch River through Juliaetta -- because it had to lose roughly 1,800 feet off the Palouse prairie to reach the Clearwater. Compare the water-level Union Pacific approach up the Snake ten years later, in owi_riparia_lewiston.geojson. Drawn from Moscow; the branch actually originated further north at Pullman and ultimately at Marshall, which is not drawn here.", "sources": ["https://www.nezpercecountymuseum.com/learn-about-nez-perce-county (September 1898 the first train whistled its way into Lewiston; Northern Pacific reached Genesee, Idaho in 1890 -- note this source says 1890 where others say 1888)", "https://www.angelfire.com/wa/lctrainclub/news/Camas.html (first train arrived at Lewiston September 15 1898 over the newly completed Northern Pacific line from Moscow to Lewiston via Kendrick)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_and_Idaho_Railway (Spokane and Palouse Railway, an NP predecessor, completed Marshall south to Genesee, Idaho in 1888 and a branch from Pullman to Juliaetta in 1891, extended to Lewiston by the NP in 1898)", "https://www.traillink.com/trail/ed-corkill-memorial-river-trail/ (the Kendrick-Juliaetta rail corridor, a former Northern Pacific line carrying goods, mail and passengers, was railbanked and paved as a trail after abandonment in 1997)"], "geometry_sources": ["Real-traced from OpenStreetMap (Overpass), shortest path over the surviving grade: the Latah Trail and Paradise Path rail-trails west of Troy (OSM tags these old_railway_operator=Northern Pacific, and notes the Latah Trail runs from the east city limit of Moscow to Troy), then surviving/abandoned Camas Prairie and NP trackage from Kendrick down through Juliaetta to Lewiston. 936 points, 82.65 km / 51.36 mi.", "TWO GAPS ARE BRIDGED with straight connectors where OSM has no continuous grade: 2.57 km inside Moscow (46.7300,-117.0096 to 46.7192,-116.9799) and 5.32 km between Troy and Kendrick (46.7279,-116.7570 to 46.6844,-116.7278), the latter being the steep descent off American Ridge that was abandoned and never converted to trail. Both are straight lines, not the real alignment.", "The 1891 feature is the same traced line truncated at Juliaetta (29.12 mi from Moscow)."], "not_yet_sourced": "All frequencies are estimates, not timetables. Also unsourced: the mileage of the NP line between Moscow and Lewiston (the 51.36 mi here is measured off the traced geometry, not from a source), running times, the exact date NP passenger service ended, and the precise date the Moscow-Troy and Troy-Kendrick segments were abandoned (only the 1997 Kendrick-Juliaetta abandonment is sourced). Sources conflict on when NP first reached Genesee: 1888 per the Washington and Idaho Railway article, 1890 per the Nez Perce County Museum. One search result claimed the branch was extended to Lewiston in 1890, which is rejected here as inconsistent with the well-documented September 15 1898 first train.", "freight_only_audit": "2026-08-24: removed as part of a repo-wide freight-only audit. Removed here: segment 1970-1997 from np-moscow-lewiston ('freight-only decline' under Burlington Northern). REPO RULE: this map shows PASSENGER rail only. Freight-only operation is not represented -- a line's segments must end when its passenger service ends. See CONVENTIONS.md.", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Spokane and Palouse Railway (Northern Pacific predecessor)", "line": "Spokane and Palouse: Moscow \u2013 Juliaetta", "id": "sp-moscow-juliaetta", "color": "#2E8B57", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 30, "speed_source": "estimated for 1890s steam on a winding branch losing ~1,500 ft off the Palouse to the Potlatch River; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1891, "end": 1898, "headway_min": 720, "note": "The Spokane and Palouse Railway reached Juliaetta in 1891 as a branch off its Pullman-Genesee line, and stopped there for seven years: the remaining run down to Lewiston crossed the Nez Perce Reservation and the right-of-way could not be settled. Juliaetta was the end of track, and Lewiston -- the older, larger town -- still had no railroad at all. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://www.nezpercecountymuseum.com/learn-about-nez-perce-county", "https://www.angelfire.com/wa/lctrainclub/news/Camas.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_and_Idaho_Railway", "https://www.traillink.com/trail/e